EXT. LANTERN MARSH - TWILIGHT

A shallow lake of black water under a violet sky. Reeds taller than houses. Thousands of lanterns drift above the surface, each held by a thin silver thread that rises into mist. The air smells of rain and old coins.

On a rotted jetty at the edge of the marsh stands SERAPHINA ACHEBE-LINDQVIST, 17, in a ragged green cloak, a lantern pole in her fist. Her boots are wet to the knee. Her hair has come loose from its braid.

Behind her, wrapped in a patchwork shawl, a figure sits on an overturned bucket: THE FERRYWOMAN, ageless, skin like bark, eyes pale as river stones. She mends a net by feel.

A third presence stirs in the reeds. A boy-shaped shadow with a heron's long legs, wading toward them without a sound.

SERAPHINA
Is that him?

FERRYWOMAN
That is what comes.

SERAPHINA
I asked who.

FERRYWOMAN
And I answered.

The shadow reaches the jetty. Climbs up, dripping. It unfolds into a gaunt young man in a ferryman's coat, face white and handsome, eyes dark as wells. He looks at Seraphina. Bows.

SHADOW
Greetings.

SERAPHINA
Who are you?

SHADOW
Whoever you need.

FERRYWOMAN
Do not trust him.

SHADOW
She never does.

Seraphina steps back. The lantern pole trembles. The threads overhead hum.

SERAPHINA
I came for my brother.

SHADOW
I know.

SERAPHINA
They said you took him.

SHADOW
They say a great deal.

SERAPHINA
Did you?

The Shadow glances at the Ferrywoman. She does not look up from her net.

SHADOW
Your brother is a lantern. Third row, left of the willow.

SERAPHINA
A lantern?

SHADOW
They all are, eventually.

Seraphina looks out at the thousands of lights. They sway, gentle, indifferent.

SERAPHINA
I want him back.

SHADOW
I can make an exchange.

FERRYWOMAN
Seraphina.

SERAPHINA
What exchange?

SHADOW
A year of your life.

SERAPHINA
A year.

SHADOW
For a year of his.

The Ferrywoman drops her net. It slides off her knee into the water, and sinks.

FERRYWOMAN
(low)
Listen to me, child. He is not what he says.

SHADOW
I am exactly what I say.

FERRYWOMAN
Then tell her your name.

The Shadow smiles for the first time. A smile like a closing door.

SHADOW
Orren.

At the word, every lantern on the marsh flares, a thousand white flames at once, and Seraphina cries out and raises an arm against the glare. Orren stands in the light, untouched, polite, waiting.
